# Convoy server configuration — copy to server/.env and fill in.
# server/.env is gitignored. In production set these via systemd `Environment=`
# instead; server.js lets already-set env vars win over the file.

# ─────────────────────────── Supabase (REQUIRED) ───────────────────────────
# Accounts and identity both live in Supabase. The server refuses to boot
# without SUPABASE_URL + SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY + SUPABASE_DB_URL.

# Dashboard → Project Settings → Data API → Project URL.
# Use YOUR project's URL — the placeholder below is not a real project.
SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co

# Dashboard → Project Settings → API Keys → Secret keys ("sb_secret_…").
# A legacy service_role JWT also works via SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY.
# SECRET — full admin access to Auth and the database. Server-side only, never
# ships to the browser or the APK.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=

# Dashboard → Project Settings → Database → Database password.
# The direct host below is region-independent and is what the VPS uses (it has
# working IPv6 egress — db.<project-ref>.supabase.co resolves to an IPv6 address
# only). Substitute your database password; everything else is already correct.
#   postgresql://postgres:PASSWORD@db.<project-ref>.supabase.co:5432/postgres
# On an IPv4-only host use the pooler string from the dashboard instead
# (Connection string → URI → Session pooler), which includes your region.
SUPABASE_DB_URL=

# Optional. Only needed if the project still signs JWTs with the legacy HS256
# shared secret. This project uses asymmetric ES256 keys, so leave it blank.
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=

# Optional. Convoy is username-only, so every account gets a synthetic address
# <username>@<domain> to satisfy GoTrue. It never receives mail — do NOT point
# this at a domain you actually run.
# AUTH_EMAIL_DOMAIN=users.convoy.local

# RECOMMENDED. The Supabase CA cert (Dashboard → Settings → Database → SSL
# configuration), either as a path or as the PEM text itself. WITHOUT IT the DB
# connection is encrypted but its certificate chain is NOT verified, which leaves
# it open to an active attacker on the path to the database. The server warns
# about this at every boot.
# SUPABASE_DB_CA=/etc/convoy/supabase-ca.crt

# Set this in production once SUPABASE_DB_CA is in place: it turns "no CA
# configured" from a warning nobody reads into a refusal to boot.
# SUPABASE_DB_STRICT_TLS=1

# Supabase Auth rate-limits /token BY IP ADDRESS (token bucket, capacity 30).
# Every GoTrue call comes from this server, so unless the caller's real address
# is forwarded, EVERY rider shares one bucket — a few dozen sign-ins or hourly
# session refreshes and everyone starts getting 429s. The server therefore sends
# Sb-Forwarded-For automatically whenever SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY is a modern
# sb_secret_… key (legacy anon/service_role JWTs are not supported for this).
#
# For it to actually take effect you must ALSO switch on "IP Address Forwarding"
# under Dashboard → Authentication → Rate Limits — it is off by default on new
# projects. Sending the header while it is off is harmless; GoTrue ignores it.
# Set this to 0 only to suppress the header entirely.
# SUPABASE_FORWARD_IP=0

# Optional tuning.
# SUPABASE_DB_POOL=8
# SUPABASE_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# ──────────────────────── Email (Resend) — OPTIONAL ────────────────────────
# Leave RESEND_API_KEY blank and Convoy behaves exactly as it did before email
# support: username + password, with a one-shot recovery code as the only reset
# path. Set it and these unlock, all of them additive:
#   · register with an email address and confirm it
#   · sign in with the email instead of the username
#   · reset the password by emailed link
#   · sign in with no password at all (a link mailed to a confirmed address)
#   · change the address on an account
#
# Convoy sends its own mail and does NOT use Supabase's SMTP settings — nothing
# needs configuring under Dashboard → Authentication → Emails.

# Optional. AES-256 key (64 hex chars or base64, 32 bytes) encrypting the
# email_tokens.payload column at rest — the magic-link flow stores a live
# single-use GoTrue credential there, and this keeps a database dump from
# handing it over. Unset = a key is generated once and kept in DATA_DIR
# (.payload-key); set it explicitly when multiple processes must agree.
# TOKEN_PAYLOAD_KEY=

# Dashboard → API Keys. SECRET — server-side only.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Must be an address on a domain you have VERIFIED in Resend (Domains → Add).
# An unverified domain means every send is rejected with a 403.
MAIL_FROM=Convoy <no-reply@odoreeci.tech>

# Optional: where replies go. Unset means replies bounce off the no-reply box.
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=support@odoreeci.tech

# The origin every emailed link and OAuth redirect points back at. REQUIRED
# whenever RESEND_API_KEY or OAUTH_PROVIDERS is set, and deliberately NOT taken
# from the request's Host header — otherwise anyone who can set Host chooses
# where a password-reset link lands. No trailing slash.
APP_URL=https://odoreeci.tech

# ─────────────────── Social sign-in (Supabase OAuth) ───────────────────
# Comma list, from: google, apple, github, discord, facebook, azure, twitter,
# twitch, spotify, gitlab, linkedin_oidc. Default is google.
#
# Listing one here is only half of it. Each also needs, in the Supabase
# dashboard:
#   1. Authentication → Providers → <provider> → enabled, with the client ID and
#      secret from that provider's own console.
#   2. Authentication → URL Configuration → Redirect URLs → add  APP_URL/**
#      (the wildcard matters: the callback carries a query string).
#   3. In the provider's console, the authorised redirect URI is SUPABASE's, not
#      ours: https://<project-ref>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback
#
# Leave this blank to offer no social sign-in at all.
# OAUTH_PROVIDERS=google

# Where the ANDROID APP's sign-in comes back to. The Capacitor WebView runs at
# https://localhost, which no provider can redirect to, so the APK's round-trip
# ends at a scheme the app claims in its manifest instead of at APP_URL.
#
# Only change this if you also change BOTH of:
#   android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml   (the VIEW intent-filter)
#   android/app/src/main/java/.../AuthPlugin.java  (SCHEME / HOST)
# and re-add it under Authentication → URL Configuration → Redirect URLs.
# The default matches the shipped APK. A running server reports the value it is
# using at GET /api/auth/config, so it can be checked rather than guessed.
# OAUTH_NATIVE_REDIRECT=tech.odoreeci.convoy://auth

# ─────────────── Android App Links (mailed links open the app) ───────────────
# The server publishes /.well-known/assetlinks.json, which is what lets the APK
# claim the /l/… links Convoy emails (password reset, email confirmation,
# sign-in link, email change) instead of them opening in a browser.
#
# A fingerprint is the SHA-256 of a signing CERTIFICATE — a public value, meant
# to be published. This is not a secret.
#
# The default is the release keystore in android/. IF THE APP GOES THROUGH GOOGLE
# PLAY WITH PLAY APP SIGNING, Play re-signs the upload and installs carry PLAY's
# certificate, not this one — links will open in the browser for every Play
# install while working fine for a sideloaded APK. Fix by listing both, comma
# separated (Play Console → Setup → App integrity → App signing key certificate).
#
# Leave unset to use the default. Set to an empty value to stop publishing the
# file entirely, which turns App Links off — mailed links then open in the
# browser, which still works.
# ANDROID_CERT_SHA256=AA:BB:...,11:22:...
# ANDROID_PACKAGE=tech.odoreeci.convoy

# iOS Universal Links (the same four mailed links, opened in the iPhone app).
# Apple's app identifier is <TEAM ID>.<bundle id>; the Team ID is on
# developer.apple.com → Membership. Unset = no apple-app-site-association file
# is served and the links open in Safari, which still works.
# APPLE_APP_IDS=ABCDE12345.tech.odoreeci.convoy

# ─────────────────────────── Map / data sources ───────────────────────────
# Traffic-aware routing (TomTom). Leave unset to route with OSRM only.
TOMTOM_KEY=

# ────────── Crash reporting + analytics (js/telemetry.js) ──────────
# ON by default, and nothing here needs setting for it to work. The client posts
# crashes and a small core-loop funnel to POST /api/telemetry; the server keeps a
# 30-day rollup in DATA_DIR/telemetry.json and the raw stacks in
# DATA_DIR/telemetry-errors.jsonl (append-only, rotated at 8MB, one generation).
#
# Set to 0 to refuse every report at the door. Riders also have their own
# per-device opt-out at Profile > Settings > Legal & data > Crash reports.
# TELEMETRY=0
#
# Bearer secret for the dashboard. GET /api/telemetry/stats?days=7 answers with
# crash-free session %, the funnel, the screens riders quit on, and the top error
# groups. UNSET means that endpoint 404s, which is the right default on a public
# server — this is operational data about riders' sessions.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#   curl -H "Authorization: Bearer YOUR_TOKEN" https://your.host/api/telemetry/stats
# TELEMETRY_TOKEN=
#
# Sentry is configured on the CLIENT, not here: put the DSN in js/config.js
# (sentryDsn). A DSN is a public, write-only credential, so it belongs in the
# shipped JS exactly the way Sentry's own SDK ships it. With it set, errors go to
# Sentry AS WELL AS to /api/telemetry — crash-free % is always computed from
# ours, so Sentry can be added or dropped later without losing the metric.

# ────────── Admin control panel (server/admin.js, at /admin) ──────────
# The operator's console: search and change ANY parameter of ANY account
# (profile, email, password, recovery code, stats, PBs, friends, blocks, ban,
# delete), work the moderation queue, flip every kill switch, move the OTA
# channel, and publish to Google Play. It is server-rendered with zero client
# script and lives entirely in the Node process — nothing here ships in the web
# bundle or the APK.
#
# UNSET means the whole panel 404s and does not exist. That is the correct
# default: an unconfigured panel should not advertise itself.
#
# This one secret can change every account on the service and take the app
# down, so make it long and random. The panel rate-limits sign-in to 8 attempts
# per 15 minutes per IP and warns at boot if this is under 24 characters.
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# ADMIN_TOKEN=
#
# Optional deputies: account ids (uuids) that may sign into the panel with
# their own rider password instead of the secret. Comma-separated. Deputy
# sign-in deliberately BYPASSES the `login` kill switch — the panel is how you
# get back in while sign-in is frozen. Every action is audited with the actor's
# username either way.
# ADMIN_UIDS=

# ────────── Google Play publishing (server/play.js, panel → Play) ──────────
# Lets the panel ship a build, promote it between tracks, turn the staged
# rollout knob, and HALT a rollout — without opening the Play Console.
#
# Setup (the third step is the one people miss):
#   1. Google Cloud, in the project behind your Play account: create a service
#      account and download its JSON key.
#   2. Grant it the Android Publisher role.
#   3. Play Console > Users and permissions > invite that service account's
#      email address, with "Release apps to testing tracks" and "Release to
#      production". A key that is valid in Cloud but unknown to the Console
#      fails with a flat "unauthorized_client".
#
# Path to that JSON key ON THIS SERVER. Unset means the Play page renders
# setup instructions and every publish action refuses.
# PLAY_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=/etc/convoy/play-service-account.json
#
# The app's package name. Defaults to tech.odoreeci.convoy.
# PLAY_PACKAGE=tech.odoreeci.convoy
#
# Where built .aab/.apk artifacts are kept for the panel's picker. The panel
# does NOT accept file uploads through the browser — release.js builds the
# artifact and it lands on this disk, exactly like the OTA bundle; the panel
# only decides which build goes live and to how many people.
# Defaults to DATA_DIR/releases.
# PLAY_ARTIFACT_DIR=/var/lib/convoy/releases

# ────────── Kill switches (server/flags.js, panel → Flags) ──────────
# Runtime overrides live in DATA_DIR/flags.json and are re-read on mtime
# change, so a flip lands WITHOUT a restart. Nothing here is an env var — the
# panel is the only writer. Listed so you know where the state is:
#
#   app          THE MASTER SWITCH. Off = every /api/* call answers 503 and new
#                WebSockets are refused. /api/health and /api/ota stay up on
#                purpose (a monitor must tell "off deliberately" from "dead",
#                and the OTA channel is how you ship the fix that ends the
#                outage). /admin is never gated.
#   routing | telemetry | registration | login
#   ota          off = the OTA channel offers no update
#
# If the panel is unreachable and the app is killed, delete "app" from
# DATA_DIR/flags.json — or delete the file to clear every override at once.

# ────────────────────── Feature packs (optional) ──────────────────────
# Packs (packs/README.md) are served to accounts that redeemed a code. Their
# files are fetched with short-lived signed URLs; the signing secret is random
# per process unless set here. Set it to any long random string if the server
# runs as more than one process or you want tokens to survive a restart (a
# client that hits a 403 simply asks /api/packs again, so leaving it unset is
# fine for a single systemd unit).
# PACK_TOKEN_SECRET=
# Each pack is also a kill switch on the panel's Flags board: pack:<id>.
# Env keys a pack's server half reads are listed in packs/<id>/README.md.

# ────────────────────── Runtime tuning (optional) ──────────────────────
# Neither needs setting; the defaults are what production runs. Both are read
# once at boot, so a change needs a restart.
#
# Static cache policy (server/httpcache.js). Default on: assets stamped
# `?v=cyNNN` are served `public, max-age=31536000, immutable` (the stamp only
# ever changes with the file, so a browser may keep them for a year); index.html
# and everything unstamped stay `no-cache` with an ETag, so a revalidation is a
# 304 instead of the body. Set to 0 if a stale-asset problem is ever suspected:
# it restores `no-cache` everywhere, keeping the ETag.
# STATIC_IMMUTABLE=0
#
# Global per-connection websocket budget, messages per minute over ALL message
# types (server-024). A rider over it has messages dropped (never the socket)
# and is named once per window in the log (`crossed WS_MSG_BUDGET`). Sized at
# a racer's documented peak (~1240/min) × 1.45; raise it only if a real rider's
# name shows up in that log line.
# WS_MSG_BUDGET=1800
#
# Seat hold on socket close (server-035). A rider whose socket drops — tunnel,
# cell handover, app backgrounded — keeps their convoy seat for this many
# milliseconds; the next connection carrying the resume token from their last
# `welcome` drops straight back in, and the convoy reads "lost signal" then
# "reconnected" instead of "left" / "joined". The client keeps the roster on
# screen for js/net.js PARTY_GRACE_MS, which must stay <= this value
# (test/partyresume.test.js pins both). 0 disables the hold: every close is a
# leave again, exactly the pre-cycle-58 behaviour.
# PARTY_GRACE_MS=90000
